issue type
That is the bridge between assessment and actual ETL execution.
B. Turn suggestions into executable rules
The agent should not just say “fix nulls” — it should produce:
which columns need transformation
how to transform them
which engine to use
whether to auto-fix or require human review
Example output:
customer.email → sanitize_email
order_date → parse_dates
sales_amount → coerce_numeric
address → flatten_nested
2. The required automation steps
Step 1: source discovery + schema inference
detect whether input is Azure SQL table, CSV/JSON in Blob, or stream event
infer schema, datatype, semantic type, null counts, cardinality
Step 2: quality assessment
detect nulls, invalid formats, mixed types, duplicates, orphan FKs
classify issues into transform actions
Step 3: plan transformation
This is the core agentic part:
generate a transformation plan / manifest
choose transform patterns
choose execution engine based on volume and latency
create reusable transform modules
Step 4: execute transformation
Use a real engine, not just AI:
batch/large data → Spark / Synapse / Databricks
medium data → ADF Mapping Data Flow / SQL / Python
real-time stream → Azure Stream Analytics / Spark Structured Streaming / Kafka/Event Hubs
semi-structured JSON → flatten and normalize before load
Step 5: validate and loop
run post-transform quality checks
compare row counts, schema, sample values
flag failures and optionally re-run or ask for review
3. How to support all data types, including real-time and big data
For batch / big data
Use a scalable engine:
Azure Synapse Spark
Azure Databricks
ADF Mapping Data Flow on Spark
Spark notebooks or PySpark jobs
Build transform operations as reusable primitives:
standardize dates
normalize numeric formats
trim whitespace
cast types
deduplicate
flatten nested JSON
map columns to canonical names
For real-time
Use a streaming layer and a “stream transform catalog”:
Event Hubs / Kafka → stream ingestion
Spark Structured Streaming / Stream Analytics → transform on the fly
write into delta / lakehouse / SQL sink
For streaming, the agent should decide:
which transforms are safe in real time
which should be done in micro-batch
which require batch post-processing
4. Practical agentic architecture
Use three layers:
Discovery + Assessment (you already have)
extract schema
detect DQ issues
Planner / Agent
convert assessment into transform rules
choose engine
generate pipeline config / SQL / Spark code
Executor
run the actual transform job
call ADF pipeline, Synapse/Spark notebook, or Python/Spark code
then validate
5. Important design principles
Make transforms metadata-driven
don’t hardcode for one dataset
store rules in a manifest or catalog
Keep engine choice separate
AI decides “what”
Spark/SQL/ADF does the “how”
Support human review for risky cases
“review_manually” suggestions are important
Track lineage and audit
keep record of source → transform → target
Do not rely on LLMs alone
use them to generate rules and mapping
use deterministic code/modules to execute
6. Example end-to-end flow
Agent extracts from Azure SQL / Blob
Agent profiles data and builds DQ report
transformation_suggester.py creates a manifest
Agent generates execution plan:
customer_id → cast int
order_date → parse date
email → sanitize
description → trim
Engine applies transforms:
if Big Data → Spark job
if realtime → stream transform
else → ADF / SQL
Agent validates result and updates metadata
7. Bottom line
To automate transformation for company-scale data:
use your agent for planning and rule generation
use a real transformation engine for execution
make the process manifest-driven and reusable
keep streaming and batch as separate execution paths
let the agent decide the transform action, but not execute it directly
 
